WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.307
Reference-based metrics: BLEU, ROUGE, and their limits.

2
00:00:03.587 --> 00:00:10.115
Before LLMs, text generation was mostly scored by comparing outputs with human-written references.

3
00:00:10.395 --> 00:00:22.747
BLEU (Papineni and colleagues, 2002) was built for machine translation: it measures modified n-gram precision (how many of the candidate’s word sequences appear in the references,

4
00:00:23.027 --> 00:00:30.749
with clipping so repeats don’t count twice) and multiplies by a brevity penalty so very short outputs can’t game precision.

5
00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:44.747
ROUGE (Lin, 2004) was built for summarization and is recall-oriented: ROUGE-N counts how many of the reference’s n-grams the candidate recovers, and ROUGE-L uses the longest common subsequence.

6
00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:51.747
Both were real advances, because they made frequent, cheap evaluation possible where human review took weeks.

7
00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:56.571
Both measure surface overlap, and the evidence on what that tells you is sobering.

8
00:00:56.851 --> 00:01:15.923
Reiter’s 2018 structured review of 284 correlations from 34 papers found support for BLEU as a diagnostic for machine translation systems, but not for other generation tasks, not for evaluating individual texts, and not for scientific hypothesis testing.

9
00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:27.979
For code, the HumanEval paper computed BLEU for correct and incorrect solutions and found the distributions overlapped heavily: functionally wrong programs often scored higher than correct ones.

10
00:01:28.259 --> 00:01:29.923
The failure is easy to see.

11
00:01:30.203 --> 00:01:39.781
A correct answer in different words (“you’ll see the money in about a week”) shares almost nothing with the reference “refunds arrive within 5 to 7 business days.

12
00:01:40.061 --> 00:01:48.339
” A wrong answer that copies the reference’s words (“refunds never arrive within 5 to 7 business days”) scores almost perfectly.

13
00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:58.155
So treat overlap metrics as, at most, a cheap trend line across many outputs, never as a per-output grader or a release gate for open-ended text.
